WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:03.150
The second decision is where
passwords are validated.

2
00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:08.376
With password hash sync, validation happens
in the cloud against a synchronized hash

3
00:00:08.376 --> 00:00:11.468
of the password hash, with
no extra infrastructure,

4
00:00:11.468 --> 00:00:13.750
and it survives an on-premises outage.

5
00:00:13.750 --> 00:00:19.112
Pass-through authentication validates in
the cloud after an on-premises agent checks

6
00:00:19.112 --> 00:00:22.911
AD; Microsoft recommends at
least three agents in production,

7
00:00:22.911 --> 00:00:26.113
treated as tier zero, and it
doesn't survive an outage.

8
00:00:26.113 --> 00:00:30.963
Federation with AD FS validates
on-premises and needs AD FS and

9
00:00:30.963 --> 00:00:35.813
Web Application Proxy farms,
load balancers and certificates.

10
00:00:35.813 --> 00:00:39.000
Leaked-credential detection
needs password hash sync.

11
00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:42.854
Microsoft recommends enabling
it whichever method you choose:

12
00:00:42.854 --> 00:00:47.072
it's the fallback if agents or
federation fail, and organizations that

13
00:00:47.072 --> 00:00:51.363
had it on recovered from ransomware
outages in hours rather than weeks.

14
00:00:51.363 --> 00:00:53.187
The failover is manual.

15
00:00:53.187 --> 00:00:57.795
So the program's position is password
hash sync preferred everywhere,

16
00:00:57.795 --> 00:01:01.688
pass-through transitional, and
AD FS a decommission target.

17
00:01:01.688 --> 00:01:02.950
Then, matching.

18
00:01:02.950 --> 00:01:07.137
When sync adds an object that
might already exist in the cloud,

19
00:01:07.137 --> 00:01:10.586
Entra ID tries a hard match
first: the source anchor,

20
00:01:10.586 --> 00:01:15.512
by default the consistency GUID,
against the cloud object's immutable ID.

21
00:01:15.512 --> 00:01:19.606
Without one, a soft match on
UPN or primary SMTP address,

22
00:01:19.606 --> 00:01:24.063
which only works if the cloud
object has no immutable ID yet.

23
00:01:24.063 --> 00:01:27.523
Either one overwrites the
cloud object's attributes,

24
00:01:27.523 --> 00:01:30.512
including the password
hash, so clean AD first.

25
00:01:30.512 --> 00:01:34.305
Two tenant settings block soft
match and hard-match takeover;

26
00:01:34.305 --> 00:01:39.063
Microsoft advises leaving both blocked
except in a planned matching window.

27
00:01:39.063 --> 00:01:44.486
And since July 1, 2026, Entra ID blocks
a hard match onto a cloud account that

28
00:01:44.486 --> 00:01:47.865
already has an on-premises
object identifier,

29
00:01:47.865 --> 00:01:52.400
or holds or is eligible for a
privileged role, for both engines.

30
00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:55.990
Don't plan to take over cloud
administrator accounts by sync.
